    "rawResponseText": "Here is a comprehensive overview of the soft brand and collection options commonly considered globally when a hotel owner is looking for a conversion:\n\n---\n\n## 🏨 Soft Brand & Collection Options for Hotel Conversions – Global Overview\n\n\nThe upper-upscale and upscale conversion space is increasingly crowded, and the pitch to owners is essentially the same everywhere: keep your name, get our distribution.\n \nThese collections retain a specific property name, as well as a unique design and orientation toward their local community.\n Below are the major brands organized by parent company:\n\n---\n\n### 1. 🔵 **Marriott International**\n\nMarriott played a pivotal role in expanding the soft brand hotel concept through its **Autograph Collection**, launched in 2010. Building on this success, Marriott expanded its offerings through the acquisition of Starwood Hotels and Resorts, which included other soft brands like **The Luxury Collection**, **Tribute Portfolio**, and **Design Hotels**.\n\n\n---\n\n### 2. 🟡 **Hilton**\n\nHilton offers the **Curio Collection** and **Tapestry Collection** as soft brand options.\n At the luxury end, \nHilton also has **LXR Hotels & Resorts**, and in 2024 announced a partnership with **Small Luxury Hotels of the World (SLH)**.\n \nThis gives Hilton Honors members new options to earn and burn points, while SLH gets exposure on Hilton booking channels.\n At the economy/midscale end, \n**Hilton's Spark** was designed from day one as a 100%-conversion product and passed 100 open hotels within 18 months.\n\n\n---\n\n### 3. 🟢 **IHG (InterContinental Hotels Group)**\n\nIHG's conversion brand arsenal includes the luxury-oriented **Vignette Collection** and more mainstream **Voco** and **Garner**.\n IHG has also recently launched the **Noted Collection** targeting the upscale-to-upper-upscale segment. \nThe midscale conversion brand **Garner Hotels**, launched in August 2023, surpassed 120 open and pipeline hotels within two years and gained popularity with owners thanks to its competitive conversion cost per key, flexible design standards, and reduced pre-opening costs.\n\n\n---\n\n### 4. 🔴 **Hyatt**\n\nHyatt offers the **Unbound Collection** and **JdV (Joie de Vivre)**\n as its soft brand/collection options across different price points. \nHyatt has also expanded its lifestyle reach through its acquisition of **Mr & Mrs Smith**.\n\n\n---\n\n### 5. 🟠 **Accor**\n\nAccor has **Emblems Collection**, **MGallery**, and the **Handwritten Collection** — showing that all of the major players feel a need for soft brands at multiple price points.\n \nAccor's Global Chief Development Officer noted: \"Our collection brands across Luxury & Lifestyle allow us to collaborate with visionary founders and independent owners to unlock properties with strong identity and cultural relevance,\" including through **Ennismore's Morgans Originals** and **Paris Society Hotel Collection**.\n \n**Emblems Collection** has established itself as a refined luxury soft brand, with 13 properties in the pipeline representing more than 1,000 rooms.\n\n\n---\n\n### 6. 🟣 **Wyndham Hotels & Resorts**\n\nWyndham offers soft brand options including **Registry Collection Hotels**, **Dolce Hotels and Resorts**, and **Trademark Collection**, each tailored to owners with one-of-a-kind hotels.\n \nThe **Trademark Collection by Wyndham** is defined as Wyndham's take on independent boutique properties combined with a unique experience at every property, catering to both business and leisure travelers.\n\n\n---\n\n### 7. ⚪ **Best Western (BWH Hotel Group)**\n\nBest Western offers the **BW Premier Collection** and **BW Signature Collection** as its soft brand options.\n\n\n---\n\n### 8. 🟤 **Choice Hotels**\n\nThe **Ascend Hotel Collection** is Choice Hotels' upscale soft brand collection.\n It is notable for being one of the earliest entrants in the soft brand space, and \nAccor, Wyndham, and Best Western executives all mentioned Choice's Ascend as a key midscale competitor.\n\n\n---\n\n### 📊 Key Takeaway\n\n\nIndependent hotels face rising operational and compliance costs, while chain-affiliated properties benefit from distribution scale and brand systems — and this is driving more conversions and the growth of soft brands and collections globally.\n \nAs of the end of 2023, there were 601 hotels with over 101,000 rooms in 14 soft brand collections in the United States alone, with the upper-upscale segment having the largest share at 63%.\n\n\nThe core value proposition for an owner, regardless of brand, is the ability to retain the property's unique identity and name while accessing the parent company's global distribution, loyalty program, revenue management tools, and marketing infrastructure.",
